Step 3: Load the embedding model
python
from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer

model = SentenceTransformer("BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5")
print("Max input tokens:", model.max_seq_length)
print("Vector size:", model.get_sentence_embedding_dimension())

An embedding model converts a piece of text into a list of numbers so that texts with similar meaning end up close together. This model is free and runs inside Colab. It should print a limit of 512 tokens and a vector size of 384. If it prints different numbers, use the printed ones in your write-up.

A few chunks, especially ones with code, may be slightly over 512 tokens and get trimmed. That’s fine, and worth one line in your write-up.

Step 4: Create the embeddings (task 1)
python
texts = [c["text"] for c in chunks]
embeddings = model.encode(texts, batch_size=32, show_progress_bar=True,
                          normalize_embeddings=True)
print(embeddings.shape)

This converts every chunk into a vector. The shape should be (number of chunks, 384). Normalising makes every vector the same length, so closeness is measured by direction (cosine similarity). It may take a minute or a few on CPU, and less with a GPU.

Step 5: Store them in a vector database (task 2)
python
import chromadb

client = chromadb.PersistentClient(path="/content/chroma_db")
collection = client.get_or_create_collection(
    name="ml_book", metadata={"hnsw:space": "cosine"}
)

collection.add(
    ids=[str(i) for i in range(len(chunks))],
    documents=texts,
    embeddings=embeddings.tolist(),
    metadatas=[{"page": c["page"]} for c in chunks],
)
print("Stored:", collection.count())

For each chunk, the database stores its vector (for searching), its original text (to return) and its page number (for evidence later). The count should equal your chunk count. PersistentClient saves to disk, so download or copy the chroma_db folder if you want to keep it after Colab resets.

Step 6: Similarity search (task 3)
python
def search(query, k=3):
    q = model.encode([query], normalize_embeddings=True)
    res = collection.query(query_embeddings=q.tolist(), n_results=k)
    for text, meta, dist in zip(res["documents"][0], res["metadatas"][0], res["distances"][0]):
        print(f"\nPage {meta['page']} | similarity {1 - dist:.2f}")
        print(text[:400])

search("What is overfitting?")
